{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 22, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. His life was defined by complex political maneuvering and military conflicts against rising Islamic powers led by Saladin. Raymond spent his early years as a hostage in Muslim captivity after his father died in battle and this experience shaped his diplomatic approach toward neighboring Muslim states. He eventually escaped confinement and assumed control over his county while navigating turbulent internal politics among the Crusader nobility.\n\n*Early Life and Captivity*\nRaymond inherited his title while still a young man yet he could not immediately rule because enemies captured him shortly after his accession. He remained imprisoned for roughly a decade in Aleppo where he learned Arabic and developed an appreciation for Muslim culture that later influenced his policies. This period of isolation allowed him to observe the strategies of Nur ad-Din and other regional leaders before he returned to Tripoli to assert his authority over his lands and people.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nThe count acted as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not govern without assistance during his minority and illness. Raymond faced fierce opposition from factions led by Guy of Lusignan and his supporters who sought to undermine the regency and seize power for themselves. These internal divisions weakened the kingdom significantly and prevented a unified response to external threats while Raymond tried to maintain stability through negotiation rather than immediate warfare. He married Eschiva of Bures to secure the Principality of Galilee and this union expanded his influence within the realm despite growing hostility from rival noble houses.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Legacy*\nTensions reached a breaking point in 1187 when Guy of Lusignan became king and ignored advice from Raymond regarding military strategy against Saladin. The resulting campaign ended in disaster at the Horns of Hattin where the Crusader army was destroyed and many leaders including the True Cross were captured or lost. Raymond managed to escape the slaughter with a small force but the defeat marked the beginning of the end for the Kingdom of Jerusalem as Saladin swiftly conquered most cities including Jerusalem itself. Raymond died later that same year possibly from illness or despair over the catastrophic loss of Christian holdings in the Holy Land. His legacy remains controversial since some contemporaries viewed him as a traitor due to his peace treaties with Muslims while modern historians often see him as a pragmatic leader attempting to preserve what remained of the Crusader states through diplomacy.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -4.136648e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0027792894}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0008002741}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.016323391}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -2.1457695e-06}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -3.6954948e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -2.384186e-07}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -5.4836423e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.0029726059}, {"token": " played", "logp